# .semgrepignore — what a security scan must not read (W21-98).
#
# CAREFUL: the audit runner adds its own sensible defaults ONLY when this file
# does not exist (semgrep-full-audit.sh, "if [ ! -f .semgrepignore ]"), so
# creating it REPLACES them. They are repeated here rather than inherited.
#
# DELIBERATELY SHORT. The temptation with a 730-finding scan is to silence the
# noisiest paths — tests are 376 of the 450 fs-read-user-input hits — and that
# would be a mistake twice over: this repo's red fixtures are part of ticket
# acceptance (docs/TESTING.md), so a finding in a test can be a real finding,
# and a scan that has been trimmed until it is quiet is the theatre the whole
# ticket is about. Volume is handled by the BASELINE instead, which makes a
# later scan show drift rather than the backlog. See docs/security/SAST_BASELINE.md.

# The runner's defaults, restored.
node_modules/
vendor/
dist/
build/
coverage/
*.min.js
**/__generated__/**

# The scan's own output. Not tidiness: semgrep-results.json embeds every
# matched source line, so scanning it re-reports each finding a second time
# and re-trips the secret rules on the fixture credentials they quoted.
docs/security/semgrep-results.json
docs/security/semgrep-results.sarif
